artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a system of A interacting nucleons described by a many-body Hamiltonian H including a pairing interaction, and suppose its low-energy large-amplitude collective dynamics is parametrized by a one-dimensional collective coordinate q labeling a smooth path |Φ(q)⟩ of generalized Slater determinants (or HFB quasiparticle vacua) in the many-body Hilbert space. The associated collective Hamiltonian takes the form

   H_coll(q,p) = (1/2) M(q) p^2 + V(q),

where p is the momentum conjugate to q and M(q) is the collective inertia (collective mass). Along the path, single-particle (or quasiparticle) levels near the Fermi surface may cross repeatedly as q varies, pairing correlations evolve, nucleon pairs may "hop" between levels, and the single-particle wave functions change smoothly with q.

Problem. Derive, from the underlying microscopic many-body Hamiltonian H, an explicit and quantitatively predictive expression for the collective mass M(q) along such a large-amplitude collective path, and identify and quantify the separate contributions to M(q) coming from:
  (i) pairing correlations and their q-dependence;
  (ii) repeated level crossings of single-particle/quasiparticle states near the Fermi surface;
  (iii) nucleon-pair hopping (transfer of correlated pairs between single-particle orbitals as q evolves);
  (iv) the smooth q-dependence of single-particle wave functions.

In particular, determine how these four effects combine to set the inertia against changing q, and clarify under what conditions the resulting microscopic M(q) reduces to, or differs significantly from, the standard cranking (Inglis–Belyaev) and adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate (ASCC) expressions.

